Carafe: with JumpTables, a 32-bit switch compiled the way MSVC emits it (cmp reg, imm; ja/jae; optionally
movzx reg, byte [reg + table]; jmp [table + reg*4]) takes its cases into the multiblock. The decoder reads the table
from read-only executable memory, bounded by the compare, and decodes the forward targets as internal blocks. The
jump keeps the guest's load and then branches to the case whose guest RIP equals the loaded target, without a block
entry: up to four distinct cases are compared one by one, more are found in a host table next to the block, indexed
by the guest's index, that holds each case's guest RIP and the offset of its code. Any other target takes the lookup
as before, so a changed table or index only costs the lookup. The x87 stack is synchronised before the branch. Off
by default; not used with the code cache.
MIT, as the files it changes.

